=== coop/design-corrections/native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json#/x-opensip-public-route-registry
{
 "standing": "Normative and CLOSED. The PUBLIC TERMINATION each internal decision key of the capability and Coverage-cause guards derives to. An earlier revision published prose placeholders here - `request detail ...`, `operational record` - as if they were public detail codes; 14 of 16 branch targets refused the real DomainDetail schema and none of the keys was in internalAliases. Prose is not an enum value, and the claim that those targets were already members was false. Every value below is either a member of the closed workflows common D9 vocabularies, a member of the closed public DomainDetailCode registry, or an explicit declaration that the optional DomainDetail is ABSENT with the real operational carrier named.",
 "carrier": "The public carrier is workflows/schemas/common.schema.json#/$defs/StepTermination, not a bare DomainDetail. It carries `class` plus `errorCode`/`faultCause`/`reasonCodes`, and `domainDetail` is OPTIONAL. Where no existing DomainDetailCode member names the condition, domainDetail is lawfully ABSENT - the errorCode already names it - and the internal key is retained in the operational diagnostic record. That is the existing event law, not a new allowance.",
 "originatingBoundaryLaw": "admission-and-qualification section 1 fixes the actor/layer split: `Invalid external input is an admission rejection with the existing carrier's D9 code; an invalid host-generated internal layer is a host invariant fault.` The inherited d9-exit-contract.v1.14 completes it: nonAnalysisDerivation rule 2 puts authored-config errors and failed preconditions in request-rejected, rule 5 puts HOST failures in operational-failed, and axes.domainCondition distinguishes `host-fault` from `precondition-failed`. So the same internal key derives a DIFFERENT class and code depending on where the bad value entered, and calling every retained or direct spec `host-generated` was wrong: a retained spec of known external origin is an external input, and only a host BUG minting its own invalid internal layer is a host fault.",
 "helperDoesNotGuessOrigin": "The pure admission helpers - admit_requested_capabilities, admit_release_capability_registry, deficiency_cause_faults - are passed rows and nothing about provenance, so they emit the internal decision key ALONE. public_termination_for(key, origin) is the derivation the HOST calls with the origin it already holds; it refuses an origin the key cannot have, so a caller cannot launder a configuration error into a host fault or the reverse.",
 "hostInvariantSuccessor": "SELECTED NORMATIVE D9 COMPOSITION, published here rather than deferred. It inherits docs/coop/artifacts/d9-exit-contract.v1.14.json UNCHANGED and adds exactly one thing: the faultCause `host-invariant`, mapped to the EXISTING error code SYSTEM.OUTCOME.ILLEGAL_STATE. What was unrepresentable was the host-INVARIANT subtype specifically - other host-fault causes such as host-io, ledger-corrupt and provider-protocol already existed and were always representable - because StepTermination requires operational-failed to carry BOTH an errorCode and a non-none faultCause, and no declared cause named a host invariant violation. Borrowing host-io for it would be a different remedy. PRECISION: codeMaps.rule's `total and injective` is a property of the DECLARED CAUSE DOMAIN - every declared cause has exactly one code - and does NOT require every errorCode to have a preimage, so an errorCode without a cause was not a violation of totality; the earlier claim that the maps were non-total was wrong. Existing classes, exit codes, reason codes and error codes are unchanged, but this IS a D9 vocabulary extension: faultCause grows by one member, and saying otherwise would be false. Schema, workflow mapper and this registry must agree, every inherited mapping and golden is preserved, and an unknown cause still refuses.",
