 "aliasMapIsContextFree": "Only a key whose public DETAIL is the same for EVERY origin may appear in public-detail-registry internalAliases, because that map is context-free. Two keys qualify and are aliased there: native.requested-capability-mode-not-selected -> PROVIDER.NOT_SELECTED and native.release-capability-undeclared -> native.capability-unavailable. The origin-DEPENDENT keys deliberately are NOT in that map - a context-free alias cannot express `CONFIG.INVALID when configured, absent when the host produced it` - and the keys whose detail is absent need no alias because they never surface as a DomainDetailCode. An earlier revision claimed all 14 belonged there and that the edit was mechanical; both claims were wrong.",
 "enforcedAt": "native_evidence_model public_termination_for derives the termination and validates it against the real StepTermination schema; the guards themselves emit only the internal key.",
 "possibleOriginsLaw": "Every key declares the boundaries at which it CAN arise, and a derivation refuses any other. A release declaration is never a user's configuration and a configured capability is never the host's own authenticated release input, so a caller cannot launder one actor's fault into another's. An origin-INDEPENDENT key still has possible origins - what is independent is the resulting class and code, not who could have caused it.",
 "unsupportedTypedIsNotOnThisTable": "An UNSUPPORTED-TYPED request is admitted and ANSWERED, never refused: the Run closes and its Coverage carries `unknown` with the deficiency the OWNING DERIVATION selects under the section 10 precedence. For a relation the admitted universe cannot serve at all - a compiler-free references request under syntax-only - that is language-tier-unsupported with capability-missing, and it OUTRANKS provider-unavailable. A capability the release merely did not declare available carries provider-unavailable with capability-missing only where no more specific deficiency applies, and a CANDIDATE-ONLY capability has no Coverage entry at all - its public route is the advisory native.capability-unavailable notice in CommandEnvelope.availability, whose complete ownership tuple is defined in section 1.4. An earlier revision stated the provider-unavailable pair here as THE outcome for both cases, which contradicted the precedence and the candidate-only distinction section 1.4 publishes. Neither case may be converted into an admission refusal.",
 "envelopeErrorsComposition": "A kind=failure CommandEnvelope REQUIRES a nonempty `errors` array of DomainDetail, so `termination.domainDetail` being OPTIONAL does not complete the public failure surface: a route that lawfully leaves it absent still owes the envelope a detail. An earlier revision stopped at StepTermination admission, and a minimal failure envelope built from the externally-supplied and host-generated routes refused `errors is a required property`. failure_envelope_errors composes it: where the termination carries a detail the array is EXACTLY that detail, so the two surfaces can never disagree; where it does not, the route's own `envelopeDetail` supplies one. Every code used is a member of the closed public DomainDetailCode registry. Four members were ADDED there rather than borrowing an unrelated remedy - native.capability-spec-invalid, native.release-declaration-invalid, native.coverage-cause-unsupported and HOST.INVARIANT_VIOLATED - because `a supplied spec names an unregistered capability`, `the installed release declaration is malformed`, `the provider emitted an unsupported deficiency/cause pair` and `the host violated its own invariant` are four different things for a caller to do next. Reuse was taken wherever it was honest: external configuration keeps CONFIG.INVALID and a NOT-SELECTED cell keeps PROVIDER.NOT_SELECTED. Before a Plan or Run exists no run envelope is fabricated - the failure envelope carries the termination, its errors and the request id, and nothing else.",
 "remedyKeyingConstraint": "NORMATIVE MAINTENANCE CONSTRAINT on the remedy table, promoted here from a source comment so it is part of the published law rather than advice a reader may miss. PUBLIC_ROUTE_REMEDIES is keyed by PUBLIC CODE, not by internal key. Several keys route to one code - native.requested-capability-unregistered, native.requested-capability-mode-unregistered and native.requested-capability-duplicate-ownership-tuple all reach CONFIG.INVALID under external-configuration, and all three reach native.capability-spec-invalid as the externally-supplied-spec envelope detail - so ONE string must remain true for EVERY key that reaches that code, and a key whose remedy would be a DIFFERENT next step may not simply reuse the code. THIS IS CURRENTLY SATISFIED, not merely intended: the two strings widened for the ownership tuple state both the unregistered-name condition and the one-row-per-tuple condition, and the default-construction path added later routes to the same key and is covered by the same two sentences plus HOST.INVARIANT_VIOLATED, which is true of a host emitting two rows for one cell. The structural alternative - keying the remedy by (code, internal key) - is deliberately NOT taken: it changes the shape of an existing table for a case honest sentences already cover, and no key in this composition needs it. What is owed instead is exact: adding a key that routes to an existing code obliges its author to check that code's remedy is a true next step for the new condition and to widen it or choose another code if it is not.",
 "subjectNormalization": "The guards emit COLON-SUFFIXED refusal strings - native.requested-capability-unregistered:made-up-capability, native.coverage-cause-not-for-deficiency:input-closure-incomplete:capability-missing - so a normalizer matching the whole string would find no registered key. normalize_internal_key matches the LONGEST registered key followed by a colon and takes the remainder as the subject, which is what reaches DomainDetail.subject so a caller sees WHICH value was refused. A raw string matching no registered key REFUSES (native.public-route-key-unregistered) rather than passing through, as the public registry aliasRule requires of an unknown key; an origin a key cannot have refuses too (native.public-route-origin-not-possible).",
 "subjectBoundLaw": "DomainDetail.subject is BoundedText (1024) while a refused VALUE can be far longer: a structurally valid languageMode may be 4096 characters, and the composed subject then reached 4149 and made the whole failure envelope schema-invalid at termination.domainDetail.subject or errors[0].subject. bounded_subject elides: the registered KEY is preserved verbatim - it selects the code, the class and the origin - and only the offending value is elided, keeping a leading window plus the SHA-256 of the untruncated subject's UTF-8 bytes. UNITS, because the two operations differ: `raw` is the composed subject as an admitted UNICODE SCALAR STRING, and length and slicing count UNICODE CODE POINTS - what BoundedText's JSON Schema maxLength counts - not UTF-8 bytes and not UTF-16 code units; the SHA-256 input is exactly raw encoded as UTF-8 with NO additional normalization, rendered as 64 lowercase hex characters after the ASCII marker `...#sha256:`, giving exactly 1024 code points. A non-ASCII value can therefore have a UTF-8 byte length above 1024 while its scalar length does not, and the two must not be conflated. GUARANTEE: distinct long values are distinguished under the ORDINARY COLLISION-RESISTANCE ASSUMPTION for SHA-256, not by any impossibility claim, and the guarantee is confined to what is PUBLISHED - the bounded subject and its digest. No separate retained store of the untruncated value is promised or named here; a host keeping its own operational log matches it by recomputing this digest. Emitting the untruncated string would be schema-invalid, dropping the subject would lose attribution, and reclassifying the input error as success would be false; the code, class and origin are never changed by the elision.",
 "successorArtifactObligation": {
  "standing": "MANDATORY, LIVE and CROSS-UNIT. Not a deferral and not an optional follow-up. Recorded here so that a later pass reading this registry alone cannot mistake the obligation for a closed item or discharge it by editing history.",
  "owedBy": "the D9 exit-contract unit",
  "what": "publish a successor to docs/coop/artifacts/d9-exit-contract.v1.14.json carrying the faultCause member `host-invariant` mapped to the existing error code SYSTEM.OUTCOME.ILLEGAL_STATE",
  "whyItIsLive": "A checker reading the inherited artifact ALONE would REFUSE a lawful host-invariant operational-failed termination, because the inherited enum does not contain the cause. That is a real interoperability consequence for any consumer that validates against the artifact rather than against this selected composition.",
  "whatIsNotOwed": "Nothing in the product source. The selected composition above is complete and self-sufficient as the law the source carries: schema, workflow mapper and this registry agree, the extension is checked against the inherited contract and its declared precedence, and no choice is left to an implementer. The distinction is CURRENT SELECTED EXTENSION (done here) versus FUTURE INTEGRATION AND QUALIFICATION of the owning artifact (owed there).",
  "neverDischargedByRepinning": "docs/coop/artifacts/d9-exit-contract.v1.14.json keeps its exact bytes. Editing the historical artifact to agree would destroy the evidence that this IS an extension and would make the inheritance claim unverifiable, so the mismatch is carried as a disclosed, attributed obligation instead of being reconciled away. Publishing the successor D9 artifact belongs to that unit.",
  "enforcedAt": "check-integration.py holds all three halves at once: the inherited artifact still omits the cause, the selected composition carries it, and this disclosure is present."
 }
}
